\details{
The model is \code{E(y | x) = exp(x'b + a_i + g_t + log(expo))}. Poisson
pseudo-maximum likelihood (Santos Silva and Tenreyro 2006) needs only
that mean to be right: the counts need not be Poisson, their variance
need not equal their mean, and zero counts are kept (a regression of
the log of the count would have to drop them). The fixed effects take
out everything about a place that does not change from day to day, and
everything about a day that is common to all places; the coefficients
are then identified from changes within a place over days, relative to
the other places on the same day. What is left that moves with the
exposure from day to day in one place and not the others (traffic, an
event, the weather if it is not a regressor) still biases them: that is
what the instruments of \code{\link[=ivfe]{ivfe()}} and \code{\link[=ppmlcf]{ppmlcf()}} are for.

The fit is iteratively reweighted least squares in which each step
demeans the working response and the regressors by the fixed effects
(alternating projections, until the group means left are below \code{tol} of
each column's largest value), starting from \code{mu = (y + mean(y)) / 2},
until the relative change of the deviance is below 1e-10. A regressor
that does not vary within the fixed effects is refused.

Separation (\code{sep}). A place (or a day) whose counts are all zero has a
fixed effect of minus infinity and says nothing about the coefficients;
\code{sep = "fe"} (the default) drops its rows with a warning, the rule of
'fixest' and of Stata's ppmlhdfe; \code{sep = "none"} keeps them (the fit
then converges slowly towards the boundary). Separation by the
regressors (a regressor whose value predicts zeros exactly) is not
detected: the iterative rectifier of Correia, Guimaraes and Zylkin that
finds it is not offered, as its paper was not at hand to build it from.
Neither Bondy, Roth and Sager nor Herrnstadt and others discuss
separation.

The mechanism. Laboratory and field studies in psychology report that
polluted air raises anxiety and unethical behaviour (Lu et al. 2018),
summarised in a meta-analysis (Lu et al. 2020); a replication found the
effect limited in generality (Heck et al. 2020). These are experiments
and a meta-analysis, not estimators: they motivate the designs here and
are cited as their mechanism literature and its critique.

Standard errors (\code{se}), all from the rows' scores \code{s_i} at the final fit
and the bread \code{(X'WX)^-1} of the demeaned regressors \code{X}:
\code{"cluster"} (the default; \code{sum_g s_g s_g'} over the clusters of the
first \code{clus} column); \code{"twoway"} (Cameron, Gelbach and Miller 2011, \code{V1
+ V2 - V12}; Bondy, Roth and Sager cluster by ward and year-month, table
2; Herrnstadt and others 2019 by monitor and date; given two \code{clus}
columns, \code{"cluster"} means this too); \code{"hc1"} (each row alone, the
robust errors of Herrnstadt and others 2021); \code{"nw"} (Newey-West within
each unit of the first \code{clus} column over the days of \code{time}, Bartlett
weights \code{1 - d / (lag + 1)} for days \code{d} apart up to \code{lag}; by default
the automatic bandwidth of Newey and West (1994, equation 2.2, p. 634,
and p. 635) for the Bartlett kernel without their prewhitening: \code{lag =
[gamma T^(1/3)]}, \code{gamma = 1.1447 ((s1 / s0)^2)^(1/3)}, \code{s1 = 2 sum_j j
sigma_j}, \code{s0 = sigma_0 + 2 sum_j sigma_j} over \code{j = 1..n}, \code{n = [4 (T /
100)^(2/9)]}, \code{T} the most days of a unit and \code{sigma_j} the sum of the
products of the rows' scores (summed over the coefficients) of one unit
\code{j} days apart (the earlier default here, \code{[4 (T / 100)^(2/9)]}, is
their lag-selection parameter \code{n}, not the lag); the appendix of
Herrnstadt and others 2021); \code{"boot"} (a cluster bootstrap
over the first \code{clus} column, \code{nboot} draws from R's generator, each
refitting the model). The small-sample factor \code{ssc}: \code{"G"} (the default)
\code{G / (G - 1)} with \code{G} the (smaller) number of clusters, or \code{n / (n - 1)}
for \code{"hc1"} and \code{"nw"}; \code{"nk"} that times \code{(n - 1) / (n - K)}, \code{K} the
coefficients, an intercept and the levels less one of each fixed effect
not nested in a cluster (fixest's default for clustered errors); \code{"none"}
no factor. \code{"cluster"} with \code{"G"} equals \code{fixest::fepois()} with
\code{ssc(K.adj = FALSE, G.adj = TRUE, G.df = "min")}.

Two-way clustering (\code{twc}, \code{psd}), as Cameron, Gelbach and Miller (2011,
p. 241) give it: \code{twc = "min"} (the default, their second method and
fixest's) puts the one factor of \code{G = min(G, H)} on all three
components; \code{"each"} (their first, which they use in their simulations
and applications) gives each component its own, \code{G}, \code{H} and \code{I}, the
number of intersections. The two-way matrix need not be positive
semi-definite; \code{psd = "eig"} sets its negative eigenvalues to 0 (their
p. 241-242, after Politis 2007), \code{"none"} (the default) leaves it.
